WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.080
Good morning John.

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:03.040
So I was on Tiktok a couple of days ago,

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:04.810
and let’s be honest all of
the other days a well.

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:07.109
But on that particular day,
I witnessed a lie.

00:00:07.109 --> 00:00:08.380
Or at least a misinformation.

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:09.420
It was one of those things

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:11.759
where you say “I was today
years old when I found out.”

00:00:11.759 --> 00:00:13.860
This particular person was
today years old when he found

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:16.641
out that the news stands for
“notable events, weather, and

00:00:16.641 --> 00:00:17.641
sports.”

00:00:17.641 --> 00:00:19.090
This is what we call
a folk etymology.

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:20.090
People come

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:23.390
up with origins of a word that
make sense but aren’t real.

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:24.970
And they spread around
because they make sense

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:27.500
and they sound good to people. My favorite of these is “hangnail.”

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:29.580
Cus it sounds so extremely
straightforward.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:33.770
Hangnail-- it’s a hangy part off the side
of your nail. The nail in

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:36.500
question in “hangnail” isn’t a
fingernail. It’s a nail.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.170
Like a nail that you would
hammer into something.

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:39.950
And that’s what they call
like a corn, because it was

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:41.610
a patch that was very hard.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:42.620
Like somebody had nailed

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:44.020
a nail into you I guess.

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:45.830
And then “ang” is a prefix

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:47.410
you put on something to
say that it’s painful.

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:48.870
This is where “angry”
comes from.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:50.530
So any painful

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:52.910
hand condition was
called an “ang nail.”

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:54.630
And probably because it
makes more sense for

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:57.320
hangnail than other painful
foot and hand conditions,

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:00.350
it stuck around as
hangnail for just that one condition.

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:02.350
Anyway, that’s not what I wanted to talk about today.

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:03.570
While news does not stand for

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:06.440
“notable events, weather, and sports”
there is something interesting

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:08.990
we can learn from the etymology
of the word “news.”

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:11.790
The actual source it will be
unsurprising to hear is from

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:12.790
the word “new.”

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:14.290
We took an adjective
and we noun-ed it.

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:16.500
It’s just a pluralization of “new.”

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:17.640
Like what’s new with you?

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:18.640
That’s your news.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:20.790
But over time it became the news.

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:23.210
Not like my new information.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:24.210
Just The News.

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:29.000
And we need to understand that the news
is not necessarily the importance.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.700
So let’s say for example
that suddenly one day, 2,000

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.030
people in America die of a
disease.

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:36.030
That would be news.

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:39.640
But if 2,000 people die in
America of a disease every day,

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.340
it’s not news anymore
because it’s not new.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:45.690
And this isn’t a function of all the terrible
people in the news industry.

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:46.690
This is human nature.

00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:48.210
And probably for a good reason,

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:50.340
like the new stuff is the stuff
that you understand the

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:53.490
least, that you do not
necessarily know how to deal

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:55.080
with or live with or how it will
impact you.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:56.190
But our bias for

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:58.870
the new can break us a little bit.

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:01.770
Like it can make it so we are not properly understanding the world.

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:02.770
This definitely hurts us

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:05.780
when it comes to how we
interface with ongoing crises.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:07.760
As an example, in most of the
places where this video is

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:10.240
being watched, the number of
infants who die per thousand

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:13.530
births is below 6. In Sierra Leone,
it is 80.

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:14.900
And I understand sometimes

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:16.900
now wanting to sit with a statistic
like that.

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:20.950
It is kinda devastating. And it is also not news.

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:26.750
In fact, it is down from 160 deaths per thousand when I was born in 1980.

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:27.750
And if you want to talk

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:31.780
about it in terms of news, going
from 16% infant mortality to 8%

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:36.240
infant mortality is
good news. Regardless, 8% infant

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:39.990
mortality is a bad situation. John, humans have a lot of

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:43.690
biases that prevent us from
understanding our world correctly.

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:45.020
That’s just a thing.

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:46.040
And personally I didn’t get this

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:49.740
until you like spent the time with
me and helped me understand

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:53.460
that we are just bad at dealing with
ongoing long term crises.

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:55.880
But because you did that,
and you also did the work to

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:59.060
figure out the best ways to
interface with problems like these,

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:03.120
we now have raised 10s of
millions of dollars for Partners in

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.421
Health. And if you have donated
or you’ve been a member of the

00:03:05.421 --> 00:03:07.200
Awesome Socks Club or have
bought something during

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:09.050
Pizzamas or have just been a
member of this community,

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:10.390
you have been a part of that.

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:11.390
Because that’s what strong

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:15.690
community can do, it can look
past what is new to see what is important.

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:17.500
And yes, Pizzamas
starts monday in which John

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:21.550
and I make videos like we did
in 2007. 5 days a week.

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:23.550
Am I excited or overwhelmed?

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:24.550
Porque
no los dos?

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:28.660
Honestly I am extremely excited and I am doing a thing. So, we have text message updates

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:30.660
this year. You can sign up there’s a
link in the description.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:32.959
People who sign up for that, you will get
messages from me throughout

00:03:32.959 --> 00:03:36.420
Pizzamas but then never again. So don’t worry about that it will not

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:37.420
be an ongoing thing.

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:38.940
Everyday
you will get one pizza-themed

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:39.940
Dad joke from me.

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:42.350
I will make this happen.
 I’ve got a fair number.

00:03:42.350 --> 00:03:43.520
I’m not all the way there.

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:44.520
I’ll get there.

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:45.520
But also if you sign up for the

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:48.290
text you might some, uh, special
access to some stuff.

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:50.780
And if you want to get the text message
pop-up every time you can open

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:55.000
up Pizzamas.com in an incognito
browser. Which I know is a very,

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:56.240
very weird sentence.

00:03:56.240 --> 00:03:58.510
John,
I’ll see you Tuesday.

00:03:58.510 --> 00:03:59.510
Monday!

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:00.010
Monday!

